Looking at the shape, the dot sits in the fourth square of the horizontal row. Checking how the pieces must tile the L‑shape, the only piece that can cover that square (together with the half‑square triangle next to it, shown by the dashed diagonal) is piece D – a square with a right triangle attached. Placing D there puts its square part exactly on the dotted cell, and the remaining pieces A, B, C, E then fill the rest of the figure (the vertical arm and the left end of the row).

Therefore, the piece lying on the dot is D.

\boxed{D}
